The central CRO asymmetry — High

The user furthest from purchase is asked to work the hardest. A quote-in-hand homeowner reaches capture in 3 taps. A homeowner who needs a quote — the audience the primary-toned entry button is aimed at — must complete 14. The curious user faces ~20. Confirmed

The long path is not padding: every node collects something a contractor brief would want. But six nodes (zip, project_scope, openings, budget, timing, and stakes) sit after the recap — after the user has been told "Let me make sure I have this right" and has confirmed. That framing implies the conversation is concluding; five more questions then follow before contact is requested.

