CRO Strengths
These are working and should survive any optimization sprint intact.

1 · The high-intent shortcut is genuinely excellent
"Upload first" (tone: "quiet") sits inside the have_concern list and jumps straight to phone, skipping detail and name. A homeowner holding a quote reaches capture in three taps. It is correctly de-emphasized so it does not cannibalize the qualifying question from users who benefit from it. wmChatContent.ts:174, 713 Confirmed

2 · Reason-why adaptation is real, not cosmetic
familyAcknowledgement() returns a distinct, substantive paragraph per reason, and the detail node that follows is a different question set per reason — seven bespoke option groups, not one generic list re-labelled. wmChatContent.ts:324–343, 365–382 Confirmed

3 · The recap earns trust before the ask
recapText() reflects the user's own answers back in their own option language and asks "Did I get that right?", with a working correction path that replays and rebuilds the downstream path rather than leaving stale state. The rebuild is careful: it preserves a still-valid detail answer when the reason is unchanged and discards it otherwise. wmChatContent.ts:345–363; wmChatReducer.ts:314–376, 415–490 Confirmed

4 · Value is delivered before contact is requested
WmChatProjectBriefPreview renders a real, deterministic brief at the phone node — requirements, questions, and scope derived from the answers. The user sees the product of their effort before being asked for a number. WmChatPage.tsx:156–167 Confirmed

5 · Consent is handled better than most funnels
Full TCPA language — automated texts, artificial/prerecorded voice, message and data rates, not a condition of purchase, revocation including STOP, links to Privacy and Terms — sits in a footer visible at every step, not buried at submit. A short restatement sits beside the input and is wired to it via aria-describedby. The payload asserts service consent and explicitly declines to claim marketing consent. Conversation:725, 732–734, 772–780; WmChatPage.tsx:235–237 Confirmed

6 · Anti-overclaim discipline throughout the copy
The power cards actively undercut themselves where a lesser funnel would overclaim: "A comparison target, not a savings guarantee." · "Clear questions, not contractor accusations or legal advice." · "If the sample is thin, I say so. Pretend certainty isn't one of my powers." Plus the persistent disclaimer naming what WindowMan is not. wmChatContent.ts:286–302; Conversation:781–783 Confirmed

7 · The privacy-conscious resume record
The snapshot stores only schemaVersion, savedAtMs, entryIntent, currentNodeId, and option-ID history — and rewinds to the first typed-input node, so ZIP, name, phone, email, and free text never touch localStorage. 24-hour TTL with strict shape parsing on read. This is a well-designed privacy boundary. wmChatResume.ts:24–31, 215–300 Confirmed

8 · The short-conversation budget
Users who signal uncertainty — need_planning + "I'm not sure yet", or any detail answer ending in not_sure — skip the stakes node. The flow already recognizes that low-clarity users should be asked less. wmChatContent.ts:767–776 Confirmed

9 · Three-layer duplicate protection with correct ordering
Covered in §6. The synchronous ref guard specifically closes the double-tap window that a state-only guard leaves open — a mistake most implementations make. Confirmed

10 · Failure states never lie about persistence
Every failure message reassures that the original request is safe, which is true — the post-capture write cannot corrupt wmchat_v1, enforced by the trigger. The reassurance is backed by the schema, not just by copy. Confirmed

